.TH PORTAL 1 "2025-10-25" "Portal" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
portal \- expose a local HTTP service through a stable Portal tunnel
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B portal http
.I port-or-address
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B portal status
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B portal completion
.IR bash \ o|\ zsh \ o|\ fish
.SH DESCRIPTION
Portal creates an outbound tunnel from a local HTTP service to a Portal edge
server. The client does not require an inbound firewall rule or router
forwarding.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B http PORT
Expose a local HTTP service. The argument may be a port, :port, or host:port.
.TP
.B status
Query the local agent for active streams, uptime, and transferred bytes.
.TP
.B completion SHELL
Write a completion script for bash, zsh, or fish to standard output.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-subdomain NAME
Request a stable DNS label for the tunnel.
.TP
.B \-\-server HOST:PORT
Set the Portal control-plane address.
.TP
.B \-t, \-\-token TOKEN
Set the tunnel authentication token.
.TP
.B \-\-host-header rewrite
Rewrite the upstream Host header for localhost applications.
.TP
.B \-\-inspector-addr 127.0.0.1:4040
Set the loopback inspector address.
.TP
.B \-\-insecure
Disable TLS certificate verification for development only.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.portal/config.yaml
Optional client defaults. Command-line flags override loaded defaults.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
portal http 3000 --subdomain demo
portal status --json
portal completion bash > ~/.local/share/bash-completion/completions/portal
.EE
.SH SECURITY
The request inspector binds to loopback and stores captured traffic in memory.
Treat authentication tokens as secrets and do not use --insecure in production.
